#!/bin/sh
# An example `pre_up` hook: put the modem on the SIM source netcfgd chose.
#
# **This is the bare frame.** `sim-select-gpiod.example` beside it is the same
# hook filled in for the commonest shape -- a select line and a reset line on a
# GPIO expander, driven with `gpioset` -- and if that is your board, start
# there instead. This one is for a mux that is not GPIO at all: a regulator, a
# vendor sysfs node, a `devmem` poke.
#
# **It is an example and not a working hook**, because the part that matters
# is the part only you can write. netcfgd decides *which* source it wants and
# publishes that; making the hardware agree means driving a mux select line,
# and that line is your board's -- a GPIO number, a regulator, a vendor sysfs
# node, a `devmem` poke. netcfgd has no GPIO anywhere and is not going to grow
# one for one board (decision 0150), so the two lines marked BOARD are yours.
#
# Install it as a `pre_up` hook on the modem's interface:
#
#     interface wwan0 {
#         config  = "reported"
#         pre_up { /etc/netcfgd/hook/sim-select }
#     }
#
# **`config` depends on which helper drives your module, and getting it wrong
# gives an interface that never configures.** `reported` is right where the
# helper obtains the address and writes it down -- MBIM, where `mbimcli` is
# told the address the operator assigned. It is wrong on an ECM module, where
# the module presents an ordinary ethernet interface and runs a DHCP server on
# it: there `netcfgd-modem-at` deliberately writes no report, because the
# address comes from a lease like any other, and the interface wants
#
#     interface wwan0 {
#         config  = "dhcp"
#         pre_up { /etc/netcfgd/hook/sim-select }
#     }
#
# An ECM interface left on `reported` waits for a file that nothing is going to
# write. `helper/modem-quirks` says which kind a module is -- `data=ecm` with
# `helper=at` is the DHCP case.
#
# and say which sources exist, in the order you want them tried:
#
#     device wwan0 {
#         modem {
#             sim = ["esim", "socket"]
#             apn = "im.cxn"
#         }
#     }
#
# ## WHY pre_up AND NOT SOMEWHERE ELSE
#
# `pre_up` is the one phase that runs while the interface is down (0011), which
# is what switching a SIM needs: the mux moves under a modem that is about to
# be reset, and a source changed while the bearer is up is a source the modem
# never re-reads. netcfgd cycles the link for you when it moves the selection
# (0152), so this runs again on the way back up with the new value in the file.
#
# ## WHAT netcfgd GIVES YOU
#
# `/run/netcfgd/modem/<interface>`, written before the interface is brought up:
#
#     # wwan0, netcfgd's SIM selection
#     sim=esim
#     apn=im.cxn
#
# `NCFG_IFACE` says which interface this run is for, and `NCFG_RUN_DIR` moves
# the directory when netcfgd was told to use another one. Read the file rather
# than remembering a previous answer: the whole point is that netcfgd changes
# it when a source stops working.

set -eu

run_dir=${NCFG_RUN_DIR:-/run/netcfgd}
iface=${NCFG_IFACE:?this is a netcfgd hook and expects NCFG_IFACE}
selection="$run_dir/modem/$iface"

# No file means netcfgd has nothing to say about this interface's SIM -- the
# device has no `modem` block. Do nothing rather than guessing at a default:
# a hook that picks a source on no information is the failure decision 0152
# keeps netcfgd itself away from.
[ -f "$selection" ] || exit 0

sim=$(sed -n 's/^sim=//p' "$selection" | head -n 1)

# A `modem` block that lists no source is a machine that wants its APN
# configured and its SIM left alone. That is an ordinary configuration.
[ -n "$sim" ] || exit 0

case $sim in
esim)
	# BOARD: put the mux on the embedded SIM.
	echo "sim-select: would select the eSIM on $iface" >&2
	;;
socket)
	# BOARD: put the mux on the removable socket.
	echo "sim-select: would select the SIM socket on $iface" >&2
	;;
*)
	# A name netcfgd was configured with and this script does not know. Fail
	# loudly: the alternative is bringing the interface up on whichever source
	# the mux happened to be left on, which looks like the fallback working
	# and is the machine ignoring you.
	echo "sim-select: no idea how to select \`$sim\` on $iface" >&2
	exit 1
	;;
esac

# BOARD: reset the modem here if yours latches the SIM at power-on, which most
# do. Without it the mux has moved and the module is still talking to the card
# it read at boot -- and netcfgd will see the probe keep failing, advance to
# the next source, and eventually run out, all without anything having actually
# changed.
#
# Measured on a Quectel EG916Q-GL: `+QSIMDET: 0,0`, so there is no hot-plug
# detection at all and a card fitted after boot is invisible until the module
# is reset. On that module the reset is not "if yours latches" -- it is the
# only thing that re-reads the socket.
#
# ## FOUR THINGS ABOUT THE BOARD LINES THAT COST SOMEBODY A DAY
#
# These are measured on one two-SIM board, from another project driving the
# same kind of mux. None of them is netcfgd's to do, and all four are things
# the two BOARD lines above will meet.
#
# **A `pre_up` hook must return.** `gpioset` in libgpiod v2 holds the line for
# as long as it runs, so calling it plainly from a script never comes back and
# the hook hangs -- and a hook that hangs holds netcfgd's reconcile (0111).
# Debian's `gpiod` ships no `gpio-manager` or `gpiocli`, so holding a line
# across invocations is something a board script has to arrange itself.
#
# **Releasing an expander line does not return the pin to an input.** On a
# `pca953x` both the direction register and the output latch keep their values,
# which is what makes set-then-release work at all. It also means **a crash
# does not fail safe**: the mux stays driven whichever way it was last set. A
# script here should check the line is still an output after releasing it,
# because everything downstream is meaningless if that stops being true.
#
# **The polarity is not readable from the schematic.** On that board the reset
# line reaches the module through an inverting transistor, so the net name
# gives the wrong answer; the select line's sense was established by selecting
# with the socket *empty*, where a card that answered could only have been the
# embedded one. A net name and a sheet annotation are design intent, and this
# is one of the few places where guessing wrong is silent.
#
# **The mux selection survives a reboot and there is no out-of-band way to
# change it.** A unit that comes up on a source with no subscription, and never
# tries the other, is a unit somebody has to physically visit. That is the
# failure netcfgd's ordered `sim` list exists for, and it is why the list is
# worth filling in even when one source is obviously the right one today: an
# eSIM subscription can be activated months after a unit ships.
